   "text": "I read the structure region by region and wrote it as SMILES. The pieces, left to right:\n\n- **Left end:** a 2-hydroxyphenyl on a 3-aminopyridazine, with a 3,8-diazabicyclo[3.2.1]octane attached at pyridazine C4. The bicyclic N3 carries the pyridazine, and the bridging N8 carries the pyridine. The bridgeheads have plain bonds, so I gave them no stereo.\n- **Pyridine:** attached at C4 to N8 and bearing an O at C2.\n- **Cyclobutane:** a 1,3-dioxy cyclobutane. One O is on a wedge and the other on a hash, so they are trans. I worked out the @/@@ labels from the drawn geometry. The first O links to the pyridine and the second to the piperidine.\n- **Piperidine:** a 4-oxy piperidine whose N carries a CH2CH2\u2013O linker to the isoxazole.\n- **Isoxazole:** 3-alkoxy, with the 5-position bonded to a CH that bears an isopropyl group on a hash. That CH is acylated by the proline N.\n- **Hydroxyproline:** the OH carbon is hashed and C2 has a wedge to the amide carbonyl. This gives the usual (2S,4R) VHL-type pattern, `N1C[C@H](O)C[C@H]1C(=O)`.\n- **Amide side chain:** the proline amide NH is attached to a CH with a wedged chain, (CH2)3C(=O)NH2, and a phenyl. That phenyl is para-substituted with 4-methylthiazol-5-yl.\n\nI assigned each stereocentre's @/@@ from the wedge and hash geometry, then checked ring-closure digits and atom counts against the drawing.\n\nSMILES: Nc1nnc(-c2ccccc2O)cc1N1CC2CCC(C1)N2c1ccnc(c1)O[C@H]1C[C@H](OC2CCN(CC2)CCOc2cc(on2)[C@H](C(C)C)C(=O)N2C[C@H](O)C[C@H]2C(=O)N[C@H](c2ccc(cc2)-c2scnc2C)CCCC(N)=O)C1"
